{"seconds": 216.6, "exit": 0, "final": "After Qh5 Qxh5, Black has gxf6 (taking the knight). So the exchange is: Nf6+ Kh8 Qh5 Qxh5 gxf6, and material changes are roughly equal again (Q vs N+P exchange). That's worse than Qxf6 Rxf6.\n\nOK so the key insight is: Nf6+ is the best move because:\n1. If Qxf6, then Rxf6 wins the queen (net +6 material: queen for knight).\n2. If Kh8, then Qxg7+ Kxg7 and White has a continuing attack, or other moves like Qh5.\n\nSince Qxf6 Rxf6 wins the queen outright, Nf6+ is clearly the best move.\n\nLet me write the final answer:\n\n**Best move:** Nf6+\n\n**Main line:** Nf6+ Qxf6 Rxf6\n\n**Motif:** fork\n\n**Explanation (revised for 80 words):**\n\"After your opponent's queen jumped to e5, look for moves that force it to a vulnerable square. Nf6+ checks the king and forces Black to capture with the queen (if Kh8, Qxg7+ is very strong anyway). Now the queen is attacked and undefended\u2014Rxf6 wins it! You've won Black's queen for just a knight, a decisive material advantage. Key lesson: aggressive knight moves often draw defensive pieces into tactics.\"\n\nWord count: Let me recount...\n\"After your opponent's queen jumped to e5, look for moves that force it to a vulnerable square. Nf6+ checks the king and forces Black to capture with the queen (if Kh8, Qxg7+ is very strong anyway). Now the queen is attacked and undefended\u2014Rxf6 wins it! You've won Black's queen for just a knight, a decisive material advantage. Key lesson: aggressive knight moves often draw defensive pieces into tactics.\"\n\n1-After, 2-your, 3-opponent's, 4-queen, 5-jumped, 6-to, 7-e5, 8-look, 9-for, 10-moves, 11-that, 12-force, 13-it, 14-to, 15-a, 16-vulnerable, 17-square, 18-Nf6+, 19-checks, 20-the, 21-king, 22-and, 23-forces, 24-Black, 25-to, 26-capture, 27-with, 28-the, 29-queen, 30-if, 31-Kh8, 32-Qxg7+, 33-is, 34-very, 35-strong, 36-anyway, 37-Now, 38-the, 39-queen, 40-is, 41-attacked, 42-and, 43-undefended, 44-Rxf6, 45-wins, 46-it, 47-You've, 48-won, 49-Black's,